jana: Quick check — running into an annoying edgecase: when a streaming function call is in-flight and the auth token refreshes, the server closes the connection and the client reconnects. Sometimes the new stream starts emitting the structured JSON output mid-object, so our streaming JSON parser bails with EOF/invalid token. Anyone seen this? :thinking:

mike: Yep saw that in staging last week. Go client hit json: unexpected end of JSON input while trying to decode a tool response.

leo: Is this the short-lived JWT refresh flow (refresh on background thread) or a refresh via explicit reauth?

jana: Short-lived token auto-refresh. The sequence: 1) client opens stream with token A, 2) token A expires, 3) client obtains token B and reconnects, 4) server resumes sending model/tool output but the first event contains the tail of the previous JSON object, so the stream now has '..."args": {"id": 123, "msg": "incomplete' then new payload. Parser fails.

sasha: Oof. Are you using incremental JSON parsing (decoder) or line-delimited chunks?

jana: We're using incremental decoding in both Python and Go SDKs currently. Python's json.loads on assembled bytes; Go uses json.Decoder on the stream.

mike: Thoughts on options: 1) Buffer until valid JSON object assembled (coalesce) 2) Use event-wrapping with explicit frame boundaries (e.g., "event: <len>\n<json>") 3) Change server to never split object across disconnects (hard)

eva: For a quick SDK-side fix I prototyped a small coalescing helper in both languages. It buffers until a full JSON object parses cleanly, then yields it. Not perfect (duplicates if server replays), but handles the partial-token case gracefully.

jana: Can you paste the helper? I'll open a PR for the Python client and ping reviewers.

ci-bot: buildkite: started build #3742 for branch jana/stream-coalesce (link: https://ci.redwood.ai/builds/3742)

eva: Python sketch:
```python
# yield complete JSON objects from bytes generator
def stream_coalesce_json(chunks):
    buf = b""
    for c in chunks:
        buf += c
        try:
            obj, idx = json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(buf.decode('utf-8'))
            yield obj
            buf = buf[idx:].lstrip().encode('utf-8')
        except (ValueError, json.JSONDecodeError):
            # not yet a full object, keep buffering
            continue
```

sasha: Nice. A few caveats: 1) decoding from bytes -> str can split multibyte utf-8 if chunks cut in middle of char — decode with errors='surrogatepass' or accumulate bytes and decode only when safe. 2) raw_decode expects a string; you'd need to manage leading whitespace.

leo: Go version I sketched uses json.Decoder but falls back to a bytes buffer on io.EOF errors:
```go
func CoalesceJSON(r io.Reader) <-chan map[string]interface{} {
    out := make(chan map[string]interface{})
    go func() {
        defer close(out)
        dec := json.NewDecoder(r)
        for {
            var m map[string]interface{}
            if err := dec.Decode(&m); err == io.EOF {
                return
            } else if err != nil {
                // buffer raw bytes and try to recover
                // read remaining bytes into tempBuf then attempt json.Unmarshal
            } else {
                out <- m
            }
        }
    }()
    return out
}
```

mike: Will this hide the fact that the server replayed a piece? We need idempotency keys in the tool events to dedupe. Also consider emitting a special "resumed" event so apps know there was an auth flip/connection boundary.

jana: Good point. We do include event.id in tool responses but it isn't always preserved on resume. Can we make the runtime always emit the same event IDs on reconnects?

sasha: Runtime change is doable but not trivial; it needs to persist last event seq per session. For SDKs, adding dedupe by event.id is lower risk. Also expose a callback on "connection_resumed" so apps can annotate metrics.

eva: I'll open a draft PR with both helpers and tests for partial UTF-8 chunking and duplicate event dedupe logic. Also adding an opt-in flag on the client: streaming.coalesce_partial_json = True|False. Default False to avoid changing behavior.

jana: Awesome — can you @sasha and @mike on the PR? I'll review the Python bits.

eva: Yup — PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/pull/421 (draft). pinged @sasha @mike for review.

mike: I'll take a look after lunch. If tests look solid, let's get this into 0.17.1 patch.

sasha: Quick reviewer notes: 1) document the caveats heavily in the docs (split chars, duplicates) 2) add an integration test with our staging runtime that simulates token refresh 3) consider adding telemetry counters: resumed_count, coalesced_bytes.

jana: Thanks all — this unblocks the mobile app issue. I'll track the rollout plan in the PR. :rocket:
